# YYYY-MM-DD — <title>

## Abstract

What this meeting was and what it changed, in a short paragraph. Write it last,
once you know which part mattered. If the honest answer is "nothing was decided",
say that — a meeting that moved nothing is itself information.

## Attendees

Ours: <names>.

Theirs:

| Person | Role | Read |
|---|---|---|
| <name> | <title> | <what you took from how they behaved> |

The composition of the room is evidence. Who they sent — engineers, the person
who owns the budget, nobody senior — says what they think this is.

## What was agreed

Only what was actually agreed, with the words they used where you have them.
Quote verbatim and mark the source `[mail]` or `[meeting]`; paraphrase invents
commitments nobody made.

## What they asked us for

Each request as its own item, with what it depends on and when it is due. These
become rows in `status.md` — this note records that they were asked, `status.md`
tracks whether they are done.

## What this changes

The parts of our previous understanding that are now wrong: who the counterpart
is, what the first project is, which risk just became live. Be explicit, because
this is the section a later reader needs and the one most often left out.

## Not recorded here

What you did not capture — a discussion you cannot reconstruct, a name you
missed, a number nobody wrote down. Declaring the gap is what stops a later
reader from mistaking this note for the whole truth.

## Open questions

The things to resolve at the next contact, phrased so they can be asked out loud.
